# ---- Seneca Falls ----
N('wesleyan-chapel-seneca-falls','Wesleyan Chapel (site of the 1848 Women\'s Rights Convention)','Seneca Falls','cny','worship','standing',42.91078,-76.79969,'exact','open','walk','','1843',
  'A Wesleyan Methodist chapel built in 1843. In July 1848 about 300 people met here for the first women\'s rights convention, where Elizabeth Cady Stanton called for women\'s right to vote. Part of the congregation broke away in 1869, the rest moved and sold the building in 1873, and it later served other uses until the National Park Service took it into the park.',
  'What stands is the restored chapel: Wikipedia says all the interior fittings had gone and three original walls survive. Pews from the First Congregational Church of Seneca Falls have been put inside. HMdb has three park panels beside it on the chapel\'s later history (1843 to 1871, 1871 to 1985, 1985 to today).',
  '',
  WORI+' The grounds of the park\'s buildings are open every day. Street parking on Fall Street; a public lot off Southern Lane is about 80 m south-east (OpenStreetMap).',
  [W('Wesleyan Methodist Church (Seneca Falls, New York)'),WORI_L,WORI_T,HM(226594,'The Wesleyan Chapel: 1843 to 1871')],th=[42.91010,-76.79929],thn='Southern Lane lot',brick='?',hours='Visitor center daily 9 a.m. to 5 p.m. except winter holidays; chapel and house tour times vary by season'),
N('elizabeth-cady-stanton-house-seneca-falls','Elizabeth Cady Stanton House','Seneca Falls','cny','landmark','standing',42.91270,-76.78837,'exact','open','walk','','before 1830',
  'Elizabeth Cady Stanton lived here from 1847 to 1862, the years of the Seneca Falls convention and of her early partnership with Susan B. Anthony; HMdb\'s park panel calls it the center of the rebellion. The house was built before 1830 and bought in 1836-37 by Samuel and William Bayard from Wilhelmus Mynderse, the founder of Seneca Falls. It became a National Historic Landmark in 1965 and is part of Women\'s Rights National Historical Park.',
  'A modest wood-frame house, L-shaped, clapboarded, with a one-storey wing and porch to the south, at 32 Washington Street; Wikipedia calls it not architecturally distinguished. A row of park panels along the street (HMdb: Stanton\'s Grassmere, The Chamberlain House and others) tells her story. Interior shown by ranger tour; what is original inside not checked.',
  '',
  WORI+' Street parking on Washington Street.',
  [W('Elizabeth Cady Stanton House (Seneca Falls, New York)'),WORI_L,WORI_T,HM(65318,'The Stanton House: Shaping a Reformer')],brick='no',hours='Visitor center daily 9 a.m. to 5 p.m. except winter holidays; house tour times vary by season'),
N('seneca-knitting-mill-womens-hall-of-fame','Seneca Knitting Mill (National Women\'s Hall of Fame)','Seneca Falls','cny','industry','standing',42.90925,-76.79941,'exact','open','walk','','1844',
  'A limestone mill of 1844 on the south bank of the canal, which Wikipedia ties to the abolitionist movement and to the birthplace of women\'s rights. The National Women\'s Hall of Fame, founded in Seneca Falls in 1969, moved into it in August 2020 after a capital campaign begun in 2014, its third home.',
  'The Hall\'s own page describes it as the limestone building with a large brick smokestack, directly across the canal from the Wesleyan Chapel. Inside: the Hall\'s exhibits; what survives of the mill\'s fittings not checked.',
  '',
  'Run by the National Women\'s Hall of Fame, 1 Canal Street. Its page gives Tuesday to Saturday 11 a.m. to 4 p.m. and adult admission $10, and said in November 2026 that it was closed for remodeling from October 19 to about December 1, 2026, with the date of reopening to be confirmed there. The Hall asks visitors not to follow the purple signs in the village. A public lot on Center Street is about 200 m south (OpenStreetMap).',
  [W('National Women\'s Hall of Fame'),L('National Women\'s Hall of Fame: Visit','https://www.womenofthehall.org/visit/')],th=[42.90897,-76.79921],thn='Center Street lot',brick='some',hours='Tuesday to Saturday 11 a.m. to 4 p.m. (closed for remodeling from October 19, 2026 to about December 1, 2026)'),
N('the-flats-van-cleef-lake-seneca-falls','The Flats, drowned under Van Cleef Lake','Seneca Falls','cny','town','water',42.90879,-76.79527,'area','public','walk','drowned','to 1914',
  'Until 1914 "the flats" on the islands of the Seneca River were the industrial heart of Seneca Falls, packed with factories building pumps and fire engines and with homes of Irish and Italian workers behind them. Building the Barge Canal meant demolishing or moving 116 industrial buildings and 60 homes; the flats were then flooded to make the canal and Van Cleef Lake, a flooding the Cayuga-Seneca Canal article dates to August 20, 1915.',
  'Nothing of the flats can be seen: they lie under the lake. The interpretive panel "The Flats" on East Bayard Street, by Ovid Street, looks out over where they were, and the 1935 state marker for Van Cleef Lake stands in the little park at Fall Street and Trinity Lane.',
  '',
  'Public streets and the village waterfront. Public lots on Center Street and Southern Lane, about 300 m west (OpenStreetMap).',
  [HM(65319,'The Flats'),HM(65246,'Van Cleef Lake'),W('Cayuga–Seneca Canal')],th=[42.90897,-76.79921],thn='Center Street lot',brick='no'),
N('seneca-falls-fall-street-district','Seneca Falls Village Historic District (Fall Street)','Seneca Falls','cny','town','standing',42.91051,-76.79875,'area','public','walk','','1890s blocks; district listed 1991',
  'On July 30, 1890 a fire ran down Fall Street and took 87 businesses and much of Fall and State streets, after earlier fires in 1859 and 1873. The merchants rebuilt in brick, and those 1890s blocks still hold the village\'s shops. The downtown was listed on the National Register as the Seneca Falls Village Historic District in 1991. Wikipedia says the village is believed by some to have inspired Bedford Falls in the 1946 film It\'s a Wonderful Life.',
  'The rebuilt brick blocks of Fall and State streets, with the Wesleyan Chapel and the park visitor center at the west end, and the 1932-34 post office (buff brick and limestone, in the district). HMdb panel "The Historic Business District" is on Fall Street just west of Water Street.',
  '',
  'Public streets. Public lots on Southern Lane and Center Street behind Fall Street (OpenStreetMap).',
  [HM(65322,'The Historic Business District'),W('United States Post Office (Seneca Falls, New York)'),W('Seneca Falls, New York')],th=[42.91010,-76.79929],thn='Southern Lane lot',brick='?',conf='mixed'),
N('fourth-ward-school-seneca-falls','Fourth Ward School','Seneca Falls','cny','civic','standing',42.91056,-76.78917,'exact','view','walk','','1869',
  'A two-storey T-shaped brick school of 1869 in the Italianate style, with a hipped roof and a cupola. It stopped being a school in 1933 and was turned into apartments. It stands inside the boundary of Women\'s Rights National Historical Park, near the Stanton House, and was listed on the National Register in 1986.',
  'The brick school with its cupola, at Washington and Latham streets, where its Register plaque is. Apartments: look from the street. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Private apartments; seen from Washington Street.',
  [W('Fourth Ward School (Seneca Falls, New York)'),HM(65249,'Fourth Ward School')],brick='?'),
N('cayuga-seneca-canal-locks-seneca-falls','Cayuga–Seneca Canal locks 2 and 3 (the Seneca Falls flight)','Seneca Falls','cny','canal','standing',42.91450,-76.78730,'site','view','walk','canal','1915–1918',
  'The river fell 42 feet through Seneca Falls, and from 1818 boats passed it by a lock at every important mill site. When the state made the Cayuga-Seneca Canal part of the Barge Canal, engineers replaced five locks with one large two-step flight lifting 49 feet; the pool behind it was filled by August 20, 1915 and the locks were modified in 1918. Every Barge Canal lock is 328 feet long and 45 feet wide.',
  'Two concrete chambers in a flight at the east end of the village, gates as OpenStreetMap maps them. Whether a path runs along the lock walls was not checked.',
  '',
  'New York State canal property; seen from the village streets nearby. Public lot on North Park Street, about 680 m west (OpenStreetMap). Season and lock hours not checked.',
  [W('Cayuga–Seneca Canal')],th=[42.91371,-76.79553],thn='North Park Street lot',brick='no',conf='mixed'),
# ---- Waterloo ----
N('mclintock-house-waterloo','M\'Clintock House','Waterloo','cny','landmark','standing',42.90528,-76.86167,'exact','open','walk','','1833–1836',
  'A two-storey Federal brick house built 1833-36, also called the Baptist Parsonage. The Quaker pharmacist Thomas M\'Clintock and his wife Mary Ann lived here from 1836 to 1856, and on July 16, 1848 the Declaration of Sentiments and the convention\'s resolutions were drawn up in it. It is part of Women\'s Rights National Historical Park.',
  'The restored mid-19th-century house on East Williams Street, with a park panel outside. Wikipedia says it is open for tours in summer. What is original inside not checked.',
  '',
  WORI+' Street parking in Waterloo.',
  [W('M\'Clintock House'),WORI_L,WORI_T,HM(60134,'Women\'s Rights National Historic Park - M\'Clintock House')],brick='?',hours='Tours in summer; times vary by season (park visitor center in Seneca Falls daily 9 a.m. to 5 p.m.)'),
N('hunt-house-waterloo','Hunt House','Waterloo','cny','landmark','standing',42.90222,-76.84416,'exact','public','drive','','about 1830',
  'Richard Hunt\'s brick house of about 1830, renovated in the 1920s, when it took its pedimented portico of four Doric columns. Here Jane Hunt, Richard Hunt\'s fourth wife, gave a tea for Lucretia Mott in July 1848 with Elizabeth Cady Stanton, Martha Coffin Wright and Mary Ann M\'Clintock, and they decided to call the first women\'s rights convention. The day differs: Wikipedia gives July 13, the National Park Service July 9. The National Park Service calls it an eleven-room brick Federal mansion.',
  'The brick house with its columned portico on the east side of Waterloo. The National Park Service says the grounds are open to the public but the house and outbuildings are not.',
  '',
  'Women\'s Rights National Historical Park (National Park Service): grounds open, house closed. Parking not checked.',
  [W('Hunt House (Waterloo, New York)'),L('National Park Service: The Hunt House','https://www.nps.gov/wori/learn/historyculture/hunt-house.htm'),HM(60136,'Women\'s Rights National Historic Park - Hunt House')],brick='?',conf='mixed'),
N('memorial-day-museum-waterloo','National Memorial Day Museum (William H. Burton House)','Waterloo','cny','landmark','standing',42.90462,-76.86139,'site','open','walk','','1830s; Italianate about 1870',
  'On May 5, 1866 Waterloo held the first complete, community-wide Memorial Day, closing its businesses and marching to the cemeteries to decorate the graves of the Civil War dead; Henry C. Welles proposed it and General John B. Murray rallied the village. The museum of that day is in the William H. Burton House, a Federal house of the 1830s made Italianate about 1870, with a hipped roof and cupola. The Waterloo Library and Historical Society bought it in 1965.',
  'The Italianate house with its cupola on Main Street, holding the Memorial Day collections. The state\'s Memorial Day marker is at West Main Street and Park Place. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Waterloo Library and Historical Society. Its own pages could not be read when this was written: hours not checked. Street parking.',
  [W('William H. Burton House'),HM(8194,'Memorial Day')],brick='?',hours='?'),
N('first-presbyterian-waterloo','First Presbyterian Church, Waterloo','Waterloo','cny','worship','standing',42.90389,-76.86083,'exact','view','walk','','1850; chapel 1880–1881',
  'A large brick Romanesque Revival church of 1850, 104 by 65 feet, with a rose window and two square corner towers 90 feet high under octagonal spires. A small chapel was added on the east in 1880-81. Listed on the National Register in 1996.',
  'The twin-towered red brick front with its rose window, on a stone base (Wikipedia\'s 2009 picture). Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Active church as far as Wikipedia says; service times not checked. Seen from the street.',
  [W('First Presbyterian Church (Waterloo, New York)')],brick='yes'),
N('st-pauls-church-waterloo','Saint Paul\'s Episcopal Church, Waterloo','Waterloo','cny','worship','standing',42.90556,-76.85972,'exact','view','walk','','1863–1864; parish house 1916',
  'A Gothic Revival church of local limestone built in 1863-64, 52 by 72 feet, with a tower carrying a stone spire and a clock. The rough-cut limestone parish house followed in 1916. Wikipedia says the land for the parish\'s St. John\'s Chapel on Chapel Street was bought by Jane Hunt, the Quaker of the 1848 convention. Listed on the National Register in 1997.',
  'Grey limestone church with its stone spire and clock, and the parish house beside it. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Episcopal parish; service times not checked. Seen from the street.',
  [W('Saint Paul\'s Church (Waterloo, New York)')],brick='no'),
N('scythe-tree-waterloo','The Scythe Tree','Waterloo','cny','curio','standing',42.89593,-76.90896,'site','view','drive','','1861; 1918',
  'In 1861 James Wyman Johnson hung his scythe in the fork of a young balm-of-Gilead tree, asked that it be left until he came back, and enlisted in the 85th New York Volunteers. He died of wounds received at Plymouth, North Carolina, in 1864, and his parents left the scythe where it was. In 1918 the brothers Raymond and Lynn Schaffer hung theirs in the same tree when they joined up; both came home, and their scythes stayed too.',
  'An old tree on the Waterloo-Geneva Road (US 20) with the Waterloo Rotary Club\'s plaque. HMdb says Johnson\'s scythe is now completely enveloped by the tree, and the Schaffers\' remain embedded.',
  '',
  'Beside US 20, 0.2 miles west of Brewer Road, just west of the New York State Police barracks (HMdb). Who owns the ground under the tree not checked: look from the roadside, and do not block the barracks entrance.',
  [HM(151737,'The Scythe Tree')],brick='no'),
# ---- Fayette ----
N('rose-hill-mansion-fayette','Rose Hill Mansion','Fayette','cny','landmark','standing',42.86090,-76.93603,'exact','open','drive','','1837',
  'A Greek Revival mansion built in 1837 on a rise above Seneca Lake, which Wikipedia calls one of the nation\'s finest examples of monumental residential Greek Revival. It became a National Historic Landmark in 1986 and is owned and run as a house museum by Historic Geneva (formerly the Geneva Historical Society) on 23 acres left of a much larger farm.',
  'The temple front of six Ionic columns under a full pediment, with single-storey colonnaded wings. Inside, per Wikipedia: Greek Revival woodwork, a spiral staircase in the central hall, an elaborate arch between the two parlors and fine plasterwork.',
  '',
  'Historic Geneva, on NY 96A in Fayette. Wikipedia says it is open for tours from May to October; Historic Geneva\'s hours page refused to open when this was written, so days and times are not checked. Parking not checked.',
  [W('Rose Hill Mansion'),L('Historic Geneva: Rose Hill Mansion','https://historicgeneva.org/?p=174')],brick='no',hours='?'),
# ---- Romulus and Varick ----
N('deer-haven-park-seneca-army-depot','Seneca Army Depot igloos and white deer (Deer Haven Park)','Romulus','cny','fort','standing',42.75931,-76.89562,'area','open','drive','','1941–2000',
  'The Army fenced 10,587 acres between Seneca and Cayuga lakes in 1941 for a munitions depot, on land taken from more than 100 families. It stored ammunition through the wars that followed, held radioactive material for the Manhattan Project in a row of igloos at its south end, and in the Cold War is said to have held the Army\'s largest stockpile of nuclear weapons, though this was never formally confirmed. It closed after the 1995 base recommendations. White deer, not albinos, appeared after the fence went up and now form the largest herd of them anywhere. In 2016 Earl Martin bought the property, now Deer Haven Park.',
  'Deer Haven Park\'s tours pass the concrete ammunition igloos (519 of them, says its bus-tour page), gunpowder buildings and rail-car loading platforms, with the chance of seeing white deer. On the guided bus tour visitors may step into one igloo. The state marker for the depot stands on NY 96.',
  '',
  'Private land of Deer Haven Park, entered only on its tours. Its page gives app-guided auto tours in your own car Friday and Saturday 11 a.m. to 4 p.m. (no entry after 4), late May to late October (its 2025 dates); bus tours by reservation. The entrance is the visitor center on NY 96A, 2 miles north of Sampson State Park: the park warns that there is no entrance on NY 96 and no public road across the old base, whatever a GPS says.',
  [W('Seneca Army Depot'),L('Deer Haven Park: Auto Tours','https://deerhavenpark.org/auto-tours'),L('Deer Haven Park: Bus Tours','https://deerhavenpark.org/bus-tours/'),HM(96333,'Seneca Army Depot'),HM(66075,'Honoring the Over 100 Dispossessed Families')],th=[42.75873,-76.89559],thn='Deer Haven Park visitor center lot',brick='?',hours='Auto tours Friday and Saturday 11 a.m. to 4 p.m., late May to late October; bus tours by reservation'),
# ---- Willard and Ovid ----
N('willard-asylum-seneca-lake','Willard Asylum for the Chronic Insane (Willard Psychiatric Center)','Willard','cny','inst','standing',42.67917,-76.87944,'area','view','drive','','1869–2022',
  'The campus on the east shore of Seneca Lake was founded in 1853 as the state\'s first agricultural college (a state marker on NY 96A says it opened December 5, 1860 and closed with the Civil War). It became the Willard Asylum in 1865, opened in 1869 for low-cost custodial care, and was later Willard State Hospital, at its peak housing almost 3,000 patients, then Willard Psychiatric Center. From 1995 the state prison service ran a drug treatment center there; in 1995 some 400 patients\' suitcases were found in an attic. The treatment center closed in March 2022. In November 2026 the state still owned the campus and its reuse had stalled.',
  'A 550-acre campus with about a mile of lakeshore and the old hospital buildings. It is closed: look from outside the grounds only. The state marker "Agricultural College" is on NY 96A, 0.1 miles south of County Route 131.',
  '',
  'New York State property, closed since March 2022; no public access to the grounds. Seen only from the public roads round the hamlet of Willard. Do not enter.',
  [W('Willard Asylum for the Chronic Insane'),L('Seneca County IDA: Willard Campus Reuse Project','https://www.senecacountyida.org/willard-campus-reuse-project'),L('FingerLakes1.com, November 15, 2026: Willard redevelopment stalls','https://www.fingerlakes1.com/2026/11/15/willard-redevelopment-stalls-as-seneca-county-redirects-390k-in-federal-aid/'),HM(77923,'Agricultural College')],brick='?'),
N('three-bears-courthouse-ovid','Seneca County Courthouse Complex ("The Three Bears")','Ovid','cny','civic','standing',42.67667,-76.82194,'site','view','walk','','1845; about 1860',
  'Three Doric buildings in a row on Ovid\'s Main Street, known as the Three Bears. "Papa Bear", the 1845 courthouse, replaced an earlier one and served the two-jury district shared by the county seats of Ovid and Waterloo; it is brick with a monumental pedimented portico of four Doric columns and a cupola. "Baby Bear", the old clerk\'s office, is also of 1845, and "Mama Bear", the new clerk\'s office, of about 1860. Listed on the National Register in 1976.',
  'Three red brick buildings of graded size side by side behind white columned porticos (Wikipedia\'s 2017 picture), with HMdb markers for each at Main Street (NY 96A) and Seneca Street. Present use and interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Seen from Main Street and the green; whether any building opens to visitors not checked. Street parking.',
  [W('Seneca County Courthouse Complex at Ovid'),HM(305225,'Papa Bear'),HM(104903,'1845 Seneca County Historic Court House Complex')],brick='yes'),
N('lodi-methodist-church','Lodi Methodist Church','Lodi','cny','worship','standing',42.61253,-76.82254,'site','view','walk','','1880',
  'A brick church of 1880 by the church architect Warren H. Hayes, with four steep gables on its main block, a corner bell tower and a one-storey wing at the back, on a coursed stone foundation. American Architect and Building News printed its drawing in 1882. Listed on the National Register in 1982.',
  'The gabled red brick church with its corner tower and spire, and pictorial stained glass in the gable windows (Wikipedia\'s 2009 picture). Present use and interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Seen from the street; whether it is still a congregation not checked.',
  [W('Lodi Methodist Church')],brick='yes'),
